WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:11.380
Good morning! I'm the genomics outreach coordinator
for NCBI. And I'd like to introduce you to

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:20.320
the NCBI Assembly database. We'll have a closed
captioned recording of this video on our YouTube

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.140
channel. You can see the URL below. And please
subscribe to our YouTube channel.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:26.060
Tell your friends.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:34.660
If your question does not get answered, please
either e-mail webinars@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.

00:00:35.889 --> 00:00:42.460
And if there are common questions we can't get to for whatever reason, we'll post those questions

00:00:42.469 --> 00:00:43.549
and their answers on our ftp site.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:51.940
Great. All right, so today I'm going to talk about obtaining
genome sequences and annotations with NCBI

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:58.960
Assembly. In my personal view, right now,
Assembly is the fastest, easiest way to get

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.900
genome sequences and annotations from NCBI.
And I'll talk about why that is over the next

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:04.720
few slides.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:16.800
First I want to talk about the new human genome
assembly, GRCh38. It fixes almost 1,000 loci

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:24.730
from GRCh37, and there are about 178 alternate
loci. These are particularly important for

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:30.820
immunologically relevant regions of the human
genome. For example, if you're talking about some sort

00:01:30.829 --> 00:01:38.009
of immunoglobulin locus, it does not make
sense to have a human reference immunoglobulin

00:01:38.009 --> 00:01:40.449
locus, and that's why we have alternate loci.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:52.160
So please consider using GRCh38 for your analyses
in this realm. More details on the differences

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:59.840
between 37 and 38, as well as mouse 37 versus
38, can be found at the Genome Reference Consortium

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:02.640
website shown here. Just Google genome reference
consortium.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:10.780
If I go to assembly and I [sic]Google (search)
homo sapiens, I will see the latest patch for

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:17.789
GRCh38, that's obviously p3. I can click on
that, and then go into the FTP site. I'll

00:02:17.789 --> 00:02:21.389
show you how to bridge those two things in
just a couple minutes. And what I see here

00:02:21.389 --> 00:02:31.389
is I can grab nucleotide FASTA, the gff3 annotations.
By the way, we're going to be released gtf

00:02:31.389 --> 00:02:37.269
annotations probably in the next 6-9 months.
I can also see protein sequences as well as

00:02:37.269 --> 00:02:39.869
RNA sequences and their annotation.

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:47.480
Many people also want to get sequences from
other organisms, particularly bacteria. So

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:53.320
we have about 20,000 bacterial genomes that
are available for download in RefSeq, and

00:02:53.320 --> 00:03:00.040
about 30,000 available for download in GenBank.
For those of who you are new to this, the

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:06.660
big difference between GenBank and RefSeq,
GenBank are sequences and annotations that

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:11.800
have been submitted by the user community.
We do not adulterate them in any way. RefSeq,

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:21.680
we don't change much for genomes, except to
put in mitochondrial sequences. That's for

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.940
the pure genomes; for proteins things can
change a little bit. But sometimes we do put

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:33.920
in new annotations based on our annotation
pipelines, which I will show you in a little bit.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:41.220
So one might think you would want to go to
Genomes at NCBI to download genome sequences.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:48.900
But what the genome database does right now
is it shows genomes in their genera context

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:57.640
with respect to each other. So what I have
here is -- actually more likely species context

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:03.079
with respect to each other. Here I have the
several thousand Mycobacterium tuberculosis

00:04:03.079 --> 00:04:04.479
species for which we have sequences.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:09.820
And you can see a tree here with obviously
quite a few of the nodes collapsed substantially.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:17.340
Our intention over the next nine months to
a year is to make this tree totally interactive,

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:22.360
going down to the individual strain level
from which you will be able to link to assembly.

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:28.480
But we have not enabled that functionality
yet. Please be on the lookout for a webinar

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:34.760
where we will actually solicit user input
on how that resource should be laid out.

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:43.060
Right now, like I said, I think the best place to
get sequences for individual strains is from

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:45.260
the Assembly database.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:51.500
So if I search Assembly for Mycobacterium
tuberculosis, I get the very well studied,

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:59.840
for example, H37Rv strain up at the top.
If I want to look at all of the H37Rv strains

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:08.060
we possess, I can certainly do that in the Assembly
database. We can see where they are submitted

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:13.620
from and when they were submitted. If I go
to an individual H37Rv record, the really

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:21.700
nice thing is these two links to the right,
the GenBank and RefSeq FTP sites. I apologize

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:27.640
that this slide got a bit distorted. And then
I can go and I can -- if I click on that link,

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:35.800
particularly for RefSeq, I can get the FASTA,
I can get the annotations, the protein FASTA

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:36.980
and annotations.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:43.900
The thing we've done with the FASTA, and I’m
proud to say we’ve done this, is we have

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:50.780
changed our genomic FASTA files such that
they will work with popular RNAseq analysis

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:57.880
software, such as bowtie, hi set and star.
So, if you index these genomes for bowtie,

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:07.760
hi set or star, and then you use cufflinks,
cuffdiff, or DEseq, you will get actual transcript

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:12.160
expression values or gene expression values,
depending on what you're trying to do.

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:16.460
So that's a fairly useful thing to know right
now.

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:23.100
One thing to remind you guys who are doing
that kind of work, remember the accession

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:32.440
in the FASTA has to match exactly with the
accession in the gff3, or gtf file. So right

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:40.620
now I would suggest not mixing annotations
from UCSC or Ensembl with genomes from NCBI.

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:45.820
Although we do intend to make a converter
in the near future.

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:51.360
Also if things have not been submitted as
full genomes, if they have been submitted

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:55.740
just as contigs, you can get those out of
the Nucleotide database. The easiest way to

00:06:55.740 --> 00:07:03.200
dump those sequences is to go to the "Send
to" button. This is an often underestimated

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:09.360
button in the NCBI pantheon. You go to that
button, you can send them to a FASTA file,

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:10.540
as shown here.

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:16.280
I wanted to talk a little bit about our genome
annotation pipelines. These are user questions

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:21.180
I get quite a bit. If you have other questions
about our annotation pipeline, please e-mail

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:26.160
us at webinars@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:33.700
This is our prokaryotic annotation pipeline.
If you submit bacterial genomes as contigs,

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:39.680
scaffolds, or preferably as whole chromosomes,
we will annotate your bacteria for you.

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:48.620
Also we have a eukaryotic annotation pipeline.
That's something that is obviously quite a bit more complex, and you can read

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:55.920
very extensive documentation about that at
this URL shown here. Also this is a basic

00:07:55.920 --> 00:08:01.960
diagram, showing that we essentially throw
the kitchen sink at these annotations. These

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:09.500
are annotations based on perceived need from
the community — the rankings are based on

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:14.960
perceived need from the community, so if you
believe that there is a genome that really

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:23.220
needs to be annotated by NCBI, please e-mail
genomes@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov and we will consider

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:24.580
your request.

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:34.180
If you have any other questions about eukaryotic
genome annotation, please e-mail genomes@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:40.420
I wanted to talk just a little bit about de
novo RNAseq assembly. We do house quite a

00:08:40.420 --> 00:08:47.760
bit of data in that realm, although not typically
in mouse or human. But in non-model organisms

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:54.660
we house millions of records for de novo RNAseq
assembly and those can be found in the [sic]Transcription

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:58.840
Shotgun [sic]Archive. (Transcriptome Shotgun
Assembly) which is part of the Nucleotide

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:03.800
database. I can talk about that more extensively
if anybody has questions.

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:10.140
Finally, I would like to thank a large amount
of people for their contributions to these

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:19.140
databases, as well as their help putting together
this presentation. And for the next 5-10 minutes

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:21.800
or so, I'll be available to take any questions.

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:30.560
Thank you very much. I hope all of you have
a fantastic day, and for those of you on the

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:33.320
East Coast, welcome to Spring. Thank you.

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:38.140
All right. This is a pretty good question.
As a rank beginner to assembly, where would

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:44.760
you suggest I begin? What I would do is go
to the Assembly database and dive in head-first.

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:51.080
I would go to the Assembly database, and I
would look for your genome of interest, and

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:57.760
I would try to download the FASTA file and
do whatever it is you want to do.

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:03.460
One thing -- I teach a lot of bioinformatics
courses — and one thing I often tell my

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:08.680
students is, it is best to start with a biological
question when you're doing computational things.

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:11.780
Because you are most likely a biologist.

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:21.460
Do we have any assemblers on NCBI? We do not
have assemblers on NCBI . It’s a good question.

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:30.640
There are a couple of websites that outline
assemblers. I cannot endorse anybody's product,

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:37.640
so I cannot speak to the pros and cons of
different assemblers. But I can say that omictools.com

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:44.440
is a pretty good place to look for just a
listing of tools for this type of thing, including

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:51.440
assemblers. Some popular ones are velvet,
SOAPdenovo, ABySS; there's a bunch of them.

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:59.120
Also Broad has an assembler comparer called
MetAMOS, so you might want to check that out.

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:05.700
Can one download plasmid genomes? Yes you
can. We don't have a ton of them. And often

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:12.700
they will come with whatever bacterial species
they were discovered with. So they will be

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:18.830
listed separately in the FTP site. Some of
my colleagues may wish to weigh in on that;

00:11:18.830 --> 00:11:22.700
that's something we've been talking about
a lot. So if they have more, we'll put them

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:26.760
in the FTP site linked from the video. That's
a great question.

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:32.210
If there are no other questions... Oh, by
the way, please subscribe to our YouTube channel.

00:11:32.210 --> 00:11:38.510
And, by the way, the NCBI minute is going
to change from 9:45. We're going to move it

00:11:38.510 --> 00:11:45.250
to noon to make things a bit easier for our
friends on the West coast. So the next NCBI

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:50.570
Minute will be May 6th. We'll actually talk
about PubMed Commons, it will be fantastic,

00:11:50.570 --> 00:11:57.530
and it will be at noon Eastern time. Thank
you very much, everybody! And have a great day.

